NEW_PARAS = NP4 + [
 ('the empirical consequences of scalar aggregation can be measured directly.',
  'Two further literatures frame the problem. Public-management research has long shown that performance regimes shape attention: what is measured and reported tends to displace what is not, and partial indicators can stand in for whole services in ways that conceal failure (Smith, 1995; Bevan and Hood, 2006). Decision analysis offers a complementary language for unresolved priorities: when weights are known only to lie in a set, some comparisons hold for every admissible weight while others do not, which motivates partial rankings, acceptability analysis and preference programming rather than a single score (Kirkwood and Sarin, 1985; Lahdelma, Hokkanen and Salminen, 1998; Liesiö, Mild and Salo, 2007). The present study joins these lines in a public-service monitoring setting: it asks which recovery judgements are robust to unresolved mission priorities, and how much a single summary conceals about the least visible missions.'),
]
